hitectural Change 1: Introduce a Memory Model:**\n    *   Design and implement a system to represent the state of variables (local and global) during program execution. This involves data structures to store variable names, types, and values, and mechanisms\n<truncated 3036 bytes>\ne, and specify variable status (created, rebound, mutated, unchanged). Heap objects will store the assigned address and the object's value. This structure aims to accurately reflect memory state changes throughout program execution.\n\n\nThe UI will be enhanced with a Stack & Heap Memory Reference Visualizer. The stack panel will display variable names linked to their memory addresses using visual connectors. The heap panel will present memory objects with addresses, types, and values, highlighting new or referenced objects.\n\n\nThe expression evaluator's design requires a dedicated stepper function to decompose complex expressions into reduction frames, mimicking a debugger's step-by-step evaluation. This detailed breakdown will visually demonstrate each stage of expression evaluation, including variable lookups, function calls, and arithmetic operations. The evaluator will construct an ordered sequence of reduction frames. The approach is directly inspired by debugger step-through behavior.\n\n\nThe step-by-step evaluator will handle nested built-in calls. Arithmetic, comparison, and assignment operations now correctly evaluate nested function calls within them, ensuring proper execution. The goal is now to enable proper execution of complex compound expressions.\n\n\nThe UI will feature an inline reduction display showing step-by-step expression evaluation, similar to Thonny's visual approach. The engine requires an upgrade to record the full call stack on each step, distinguishing between global and local scopes.\n\n\nThe UI design requires distinct scope isolation containers to visualize local and global variable scopes during function calls. These containers will be implemented as stacked scope cards in the right panel and as scope badges in the code/teaching header. The scope isolation container will visually represent the concept of scope, where local variables persist only within the function's execution frame. The UI component will also represent how the function execution frame is removed when a function returns.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":true,\"Summary\":\"Comprehensive refactoring and upgrade plan to evolve the Code Flow Visualizer into a Thonny IDE-style interactive visual execution model with a Stack & Heap Reference Visualizer (Name -> Address -> Value), Step-by-Step Sub-Expression Evaluator, Global vs. Local Scope Isolation, and source isolation in app_build/.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"# Implementation Plan: Thonny-Style Visual Execution Model & `app_build/` Refactoring\\n\\n## Root Cause Note on Your Screenshot (`⚠ len is not defined`)\\nIn your screenshot of `binary_search(arr, target)`, Line 2 (`left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1`) triggered `len is not defined` because `evalExpr` in [engine.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/engine.js#L1511-L1522) previously matched `len(...)` only when it was the **entire** expression (`^(len|min|max|abs|sum)\\\\((.+)\\\\)$`). Because `len(arr) - 1` is a compound expression (`len(arr)` inside subtraction), it fell through to JavaScript evaluation where `len` was not in scope.\\n\\n**Part 2 of this plan (Step-by-Step Expression Evaluator)** directly resolves this by evaluating nested sub-expressions inner-first (`arr` $\\\\rightarrow$ `[1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13]` $\\\\rightarrow$ `len(...)` $\\\\rightarrow$ `7` $\\\\rightarrow$ `7 - 1` $\\\\rightarrow$ `6`) and binding Python built-ins (`len`, `min`, `max`, `abs`, `sum`, `range`, `int`, `float`, `str`, `bool`, `list`, `round`) inside all sub-expression evaluations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Architectural Overview\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart 