pp_build/`. The focus is now on ensuring proper functionality through meticulous file design. The primary concern is ensuring the `evaluateExpressionWithSteps` function in `engine.js` includes short-circuit evaluation for logical operators.\n\n\nThe `evaluateE\n<truncated 1766 bytes>\nteps: trimming the expression and creating an initial frame. Variable resolution within the expression involves substituting their values from available scopes and generating a new frame for each substitution.\n\n\nThe `reduceClauseWithFrames` function will be designed to handle deterministic sub-expression reduction. Built-in function calls (len, min, max, etc.) must be resolved within the sub-expression reducer. Concurrently, a safe JS implementation of these built-in functions needs to be injected directly into the `new Function` parameter list within `evalExpr`. This dual approach guarantees that expressions like `len(arr) - 1` can be evaluated safely. There's a need to confirm that this combined strategy ensures all built-in functions are handled correctly.\n\n\nThe design incorporates a `MemoryHeapTracker` class to track heap allocations, assigning persistent identities (like `@0x2000`) to lists and tracking references. Scalar values receive deterministic addresses (e.g., `@0x1010`). This model will visualize how references are passed between scopes, specifically showing shared references to lists. Garbage collection will be visualized by showing how objects and their references are managed and cleaned up in memory.\n\n\nThe `MemoryHeapTracker` will count references to heap objects at each step using `captureSnapshot`, considering active stack frames. Objects with a reference count greater than zero are marked \"active\". If the reference count drops to zero, the object is marked \"unreferenced\". The UI will display active objects and the most recently unreferenced object. The unreferenced object will be visually distinct to highlight garbage collection.\n\n\nThe `callStack` and scope capture mechanism is being examined to understand how variable scopes (global vs. local) are handled at each step. The initial global scope frame is established when tracing begins. Local function frames are then pushed onto the stack during function invocations. Scope frames, including all frames on `callStack` are recorded at each step.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":false,\"Summary\":\"Updated implementation plan incorporating Task 2.3 (Short-Circuit Evaluation for logical and/or) and Task 3.3 (Visual Garbage Collection for unreferenced heap objects).\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"# Implementation Plan: Thonny-Style Visual Execution Model & `app_build/` Refactoring\\n\\n## Root Cause Note on Your Screenshot (`⚠ len is not defined`)\\nIn your screenshot of `binary_search(arr, target)`, Line 2 (`left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1`) triggered `len is not defined` because `evalExpr` in [engine.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/engine.js#L1511-L1522) previously matched `len(...)` only when it was the **entire** expression (`^(len|min|max|abs|sum)\\\\((.+)\\\\)$`). Because `len(arr) - 1` is a compound expression (`len(arr)` inside subtraction), it fell through to JavaScript evaluation where `len` was not in scope.\\n\\n**Phase 2 (Step-by-Step Expression Evaluator)** directly resolves this by evaluating nested sub-expressions inner-first (`arr` $\\\\rightarrow$ `[1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13]` $\\\\rightarrow$ `len(...)` $\\\\rightarrow$ `7` $\\\\rightarrow$ `7 - 1` $\\\\rightarrow$ `6`) and binding Python built-ins (`len`, `min`, `max`, `abs`, `sum`, `range`, `int`, `float`, `str`, `bool`, `list`, `round`) inside all sub-expression evaluations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Architectural Overview\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    subgraph Root[\\\"Workspace Root (Config & Runners)\\\"]\\n        SRV[\\\"server.py\\\"]\\n        TST[\\\"test_engine.py\\\"]\\n        RDM[\\